# Every reference into the layer dimension, with what the referring key adds to it. One row per
# foreign key into `pm.layer`, and `whole` where it names the layer entire.
#
# What a partition looks like
#   This is the precondition of "a layer is a lane". `(filing, layer)` is a partition cell because
#   it is the coarsest key that nothing refines: every table mentioning a layer either extends it
#   with a second dimension (`slack` + buffer, `holder` + kind, `draw`/`induction` + operation) or
#   relates two whole layers (`coupling`, `part`). Not one of them names a piece of a layer. If any
#   did, a lane would have to be that finer thing and the whole mapping would move down a level.
#
# One lane set is a sound elimination
#   That is why emitting one lane set is a sound elimination rather than a simplification. D and N
#   are two fact tables on one conformed dimension, so their lane sets are two enumerations of the
#   same cells. Writing `lanes(D)` for the lanes D's rows fall into,
#   `|lanes(D) ∪ lanes(N)| = |lanes(D)| + |lanes(N)| - e` with `e` the lanes both reach, which is
#   the fold with the overlap derived rather than filed: the overlap of two lane sets is the layer
#   set, and the layer set is a relation with a name.
#
# The engines do not subdivide the partition
#   That is the third elimination, already made by the model. `pm.draw` has no `buffer` column: a
#   draw is against a layer, never against one of its three engines, because
#   `layers/absorption.sqlc` states they are substitutes. So D is P x L and not P x L x 3, and the
#   buffer attribution nobody can make is eliminated before any diagram exists. What the engines
#   do explain is why a lane is empty: `pm.slack` holds three rows per layer and every one of them
#   renders as `none`.
#
# It sees the schema only
#   That is worth knowing, because the key can be lost outside it. This reads `pg_constraint`, so
#   it can prove every foreign key into `pm.layer` names both columns and can prove nothing about a
#   relation that flattens them. Two places have done it: `rank/graph_edges.sqlc` concatenates on
#   purpose, because everything downstream of it counts and a count needs only distinct ids; and
#   rules have put a claim address in the checker's own `layer` column, which
#   `checks/grain.sqlc`'s `unresolvable` arm refuses. Two guards, one property: this one holds it
#   in the schema, that one holds it in the output.
-- pg_constraint, for the foreign keys into pm.layer; the grain the lane mapping rests on.
SELECT c.conrelid::regclass::text                       AS referrer,
       pg_get_constraintdef(c.oid)                      AS reference,
       cardinality(c.confkey) = 2                       AS whole
FROM pg_constraint c
WHERE c.contype = 'f' AND c.confrelid = 'pm.layer'::regclass
